  "task": {
    "id": "pathogen_heat_inactivation_v1_renamed",
    "name": "Pathogen Heat Inactivation (water bath)",
    "output": "outputs/pathogen_inactivation_thermal",
    "limitations": [
      "the tube is held by actual PhysX friction with soft-pad compliant contact (an approximation, not calibrated pad properties); the lid and tool grasps, supports, helical screw and the anti-rotation latch while twisting remain proxies; no torque or seal measurement",
      "the bath temperature is the declared setpoint of devices.water_bath (program heat_56C), not a measurement; no heating curve, thermal mass or tube temperature is simulated, and heat credits exposure only to the specimen actually seated in the running bath while the experimental clock fast-forwards",
      "the heat exposure is an inference over Graph facts from the global protocol heat_inactivation_water_bath (configs/knowledge/protocols.json), which declares no inactivation: reaching inactivation_heat_complete is NOT verified virological inactivation, no inactivation_state, bsl_required or treated portions are derived, and the declared global Safety models judge only open-container agitation, confirmed spills and incompatible mixtures, never whether the specimen was made safe, so a passing run must not be read as 'the specimen was made safe'",
      "56 degC, 1800 s (spec 3.7 path A, which cites WHO/CDC without a document reference that was checked), the 55-57 degC acceptance range and the 140 uL specimen volume are declarations or test values (parameters.test_values), not measured kinetics",
      "spec 3.7 path A step 4, surface disinfection of the tube exterior, is omitted: spray_disinfect does not exist yet; step 1 (sealing) is executed, but a tube entering the bath unsealed (the spec's S6 bath contamination) is executable and no containment-failure model judges it",
      "the leading 1 s wait on the tube is not a protocol step: the friction anti-rotation support check of close_lid reads the tube's resting contacts, which PhysX persist reports only reveal after the tube has been in the contact scope for a step, and a tube never touched before the first close_lid is refused (a documented limitation of the friction grasp); the wait costs no logical time beyond its 1 s",
      "the water bath is assets/modeled/12_water_bath (a parametric model to the Grant SUB Aqua Pro SAP2 datasheet, 185 x 203 x 150 mm, not a manufacturer CAD) placed as scenery (water_bath_body.usda, lid not in the scene); the water_bath device and support is water_bath_seat.usda, the cup, floor and foot of assets/lab/tube_support.usda standing on the bath tray with a shortened stem, because the G2 finger knuckles reach about 30 mm below the grasp point and a tube cannot be sunk into a deep holder. The seat is a layout choice, not the bath's own float rack; the bath's wall and tray are kinematic triangle-mesh colliders. Water is a visual plane with no physics: no buoyancy, wetting or heat transfer, and whether the tube is shown below it depends on the seat height",
      "the bath stands at (0.64, -0.32) on the table and the seat at (0.68, -0.32, 0.84), the x and y of the tube-seat proxy this task used before, with the tube's base 8 mm under the drawn water level; seat height, bath position and the lid's removal are engineering choices from an Isaac sweep of single runs (a seat 20 mm lower let a finger touch the bath body; 0.84 ran clean nominally and under four +/-0.2 mm shifts, see assets/modeled/12_water_bath/README.md), not measurements of a bench layout"
    ]
  },
